  "steps": [
    {
      "place": "kalinganagara",
      "phase": "early",
      "title": "The first Gangas of Kalinga",
      "native": "कलिङ्गनगर",
      "lang": "sa",
      "when": "c. 498 onwards",
      "at": 3,
      "source": "History",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          84.4,
          19.2
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "The first Eastern Gangas ruled Kalinga, the coast between the Mahanadi and Godavari rivers, from Kalinganagara, usually identified with Mukhalingam in today’s Andhra Pradesh. Their copper-plate grants count years in a Ganga era, usually thought to begin around 498 CE, though the start is debated. Early Ganga temples still stand there, among them the Madhukeshvara.",
      "today": "Mukhalingam, a temple village in Srikakulam district, Andhra Pradesh."
    },
    {
      "place": "gangaikonda",
      "phase": "chodaganga",
      "title": "A Chola bride",
      "native": "கங்கைகொண்ட சோழபுரம்",
      "lang": "ta",
      "when": "c. 1070s",
      "at": 20,
      "source": "History",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          81.7,
          14.9
        ],
        "width": 12
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "gangaikonda",
          "to": "kalinganagara",
          "traveller": "cart",
          "style": "walk",
          "bend": 0.15
        }
      ],
      "text": "The Ganga king Rajaraja Devendravarman married Rajasundari, daughter of the Chola emperor Virarajendra of Gangaikonda Cholapuram. Their son Anantavarman took the name Chodaganga, Chola and Ganga in one, and became king in 1078 while still young. The alliance did not last: a Tamil war poem (Kalingattupparani) celebrates a Chola invasion of Kalinga around 1110.",
      "today": "Gangaikonda Cholapuram, Ariyalur district, Tamil Nadu; its Chola temple is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."
    },
    {
      "place": "bhubaneswar",
      "phase": "chodaganga",
      "title": "Utkala won",
      "native": "ଲିଙ୍ଗରାଜ ମନ୍ଦିର",
      "when": "early 12th century",
      "at": 29,
      "source": "History",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          85.0,
          19.4
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "kalinganagara",
          "to": "bhubaneswar",
          "traveller": "army",
          "style": "march",
          "bend": 0.15
        }
      ],
      "text": "Chodaganga pushed north into Utkala, the Mahanadi delta held by the Somavamshi kings, and took it early in the 12th century. Bhubaneswar, already rich in Somavamshi temples such as the Lingaraja, passed to the Gangas, who kept adding shrines. His inscriptions claim a realm from the Ganga to the Godavari. He reigned for about seventy years.",
      "today": "Bhubaneswar, capital of Odisha; the Lingaraja temple is still in worship."
    },
    {
      "place": "puri",
      "phase": "chodaganga",
      "title": "A temple for Jagannath",
      "native": "ଶ୍ରୀମନ୍ଦିର",
      "when": "12th century",
      "at": 38,
      "source": "History",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          85.5,
          19.8
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "At Puri, Chodaganga began a great temple to Jagannath, “lord of the world”, a form of Vishnu worshipped with his brother Balabhadra and sister Subhadra. Inscriptions credit Chodaganga; the temple chronicle (Madala Panji) credits Anangabhima III, so it may have been finished later. Its tower, about 65 metres high, was the landmark European sailors called the White Pagoda.",
      "today": "Puri, Odisha: one of Hinduism’s great pilgrimage temples; only Hindus may enter."
    },
    {
      "place": "cuttack",
      "phase": "jagannath",
      "title": "Jagannath’s deputy",
      "native": "ଅନଙ୍ଗଭୀମ ଦେବ",
      "when": "1211–1238",
      "at": 50,
      "source": "History",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          85.9,
          20.4
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Anangabhima III declared Jagannath the true king of the realm and called himself the god’s deputy (rauta), a bond between throne and temple that Odisha’s rulers kept for centuries. Cuttack on the Mahanadi became the capital, named in inscriptions as the new Varanasi (Abhinava Varanasi Kataka). His armies fought the governors of Bengal to the north.",
      "today": "Cuttack, Odisha’s old capital; the ruins of Barabati fort stand by the river."
    },
    {
      "place": "lakhnor",
      "phase": "jagannath",
      "title": "War with Bengal",
      "when": "1243–1244",
      "at": 60,
      "source": "History",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          86.6,
          22.2
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "cuttack",
          "to": "lakhnor",
          "traveller": "army",
          "style": "march",
          "bend": 0.15
        }
      ],
      "text": "Narasimhadeva I took the war to Bengal. In 1243 his army beat Tughral Tughan Khan, Delhi’s governor at Lakhnauti, at Katasin, and the next year besieged Lakhnor, a frontier fort in Radha, killing its commander. The chronicler Minhaj-i-Siraj, who travelled with the Bengal army, recorded it. Delhi sent help, and the Ganga army withdrew.",
      "today": "Lakhnor is thought to lie near Rajnagar in Birbhum district, West Bengal; the site is not certain."
    },
    {
      "place": "konark",
      "phase": "jagannath",
      "title": "The Sun Temple at Konark",
      "native": "କୋଣାର୍କ ସୂର୍ଯ୍ୟ ମନ୍ଦିର",
      "when": "c. 1250",
      "at": 68,
      "source": "History",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          86.0,
          19.9
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Narasimhadeva I built a temple to the sun god Surya at Konark, shaped as his chariot, with twenty-four carved stone wheels and seven horses. The great tower has fallen, when is not known; the audience hall (jagamohana) survives, filled with sand in 1903 to keep it standing. Tradition says 1,200 craftsmen worked on it for twelve years.",
      "today": "Konark, Odisha: a UNESCO World Heritage Site; its wheel is pictured on the ₹10 note."
    },
    {
      "place": "simhachalam",
      "phase": "jagannath",
      "title": "The southern frontier",
      "native": "సింహాచలం",
      "lang": "te",
      "when": "13th century",
      "at": 76,
      "source": "History",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          84.5,
          18.5
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Ganga power reached south past Visakhapatnam. On the hill of Simhachalam, the present stone temple of Narasimha, the man-lion form of Vishnu, is usually credited to Narasimhadeva I. Hundreds of inscriptions on its walls, in Telugu, Odia and Sanskrit, record gifts from Ganga kings, queens and officers, the record of a court that spanned two languages.",
      "today": "Simhachalam, a hill temple on the edge of Visakhapatnam, Andhra Pradesh."
    },
    {
      "place": "cuttack",
      "phase": "raids",
      "title": "Firuz Shah’s raid",
      "native": "فیروز شاه تغلق",
      "lang": "fa",
      "when": "1360",
      "at": 88,
      "source": "History",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          81.5,
          24.5
        ],
        "width": 12
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "delhi",
          "to": "cuttack",
          "traveller": "horse",
          "style": "march",
          "bend": 0.15
        }
      ],
      "text": "Firuz Shah Tughlaq, sultan of Delhi, marched on Odisha, which Delhi’s chroniclers called Jajnagar. His court historian Afif says the Ganga king, Bhanudeva III, fled, the sultan’s army plundered the country and temples were desecrated. Peace came with a yearly tribute of elephants. It was not the first raid: in 1324 the prince Ulugh Khan had raided it.",
      "today": "Cuttack, Odisha: its silver filigree (tarakasi) is a craft known across India."
    },
    {
      "place": "puri",
      "phase": "raids",
      "title": "The Gajapatis take over",
      "native": "ଗଜପତି",
      "when": "1434–1435",
      "at": 96,
      "source": "History",
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          85.6,
          19.9
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "The last Ganga king, Bhanudeva IV, was away fighting in the south when his minister Kapilendra seized the throne in 1434 or 1435, founding the Gajapati (Suryavamsha) line. The new kings kept the old bond: they too ruled as Jagannath’s servants. The Gangas’ temple at Puri, and its yearly chariot festival, outlived them all.",
      "today": "At each year’s chariot festival (Ratha Yatra), the titular Gajapati king of Puri sweeps the chariots before they roll."
    }
  ],
