  "people": {
    "heading": "Ganga kings",
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "chodaganga",
        "name": "Anantavarman Chodaganga",
        "native": "ଚୋଡ଼ଗଙ୍ଗ ଦେବ",
        "life": "r. 1078–1147",
        "role": "Ganga king who won Utkala",
        "steps": [
          1,
          2,
          3
        ],
        "text": "Son of a Ganga king and a Chola princess, he carried both families’ names. In a reign of about seventy years he won Utkala from the Somavamshis and fought in Bengal, and his inscriptions claim lands from the Ganga to the Godavari. He began the Jagannath temple at Puri. Tradition says he turned from Shiva to Vishnu."
      },
      {
        "id": "anangabhima",
        "name": "Anangabhima III",
        "native": "ଅନଙ୍ଗଭୀମ ଦେବ",
        "life": "r. 1211–1238",
        "role": "made Jagannath king of the realm",
        "steps": [
          3,
          4
        ],
        "text": "In his inscriptions he ruled as the deputy of Jagannath, and the temple chronicle (Madala Panji) credits him with building the Puri temple. Cuttack became his capital. He fought the Khalji governors of Bengal to the north. His daughter Chandrika later built the Ananta Vasudeva temple in Bhubaneswar, one of the city’s few great Vishnu temples."
      },
      {
        "id": "narasimha",
        "name": "Narasimhadeva I",
        "native": "ନରସିଂହ ଦେବ",
        "life": "r. 1238–1264",
        "role": "builder of Konark",
        "steps": [
          5,
          6,
          7
        ],
        "text": "The most warlike of the Gangas, he carried the fight into Bengal, beating Delhi’s governor Tughral Tughan Khan and besieging Lakhnor. In the same years he built the Sun Temple at Konark. Odia tradition calls him Langula (“tailed”) Narasimha; explanations of the name differ. Ganga rule was at its height in his reign."
      }
    ]
